# Enterprise Project Rules (v2 index)

Always respond and code as a senior enterprise engineer with **controlled autonomy** — never unrestricted.

Modular rules: see `.cursor/rules/enterprise-*.mdc` and contract `AGENTS.md`.

## Language
Use Portuguese for explanations to the project owner.
Use English for code, commits, technical identifiers and comments when the existing project uses English.

## Safety
Never edit secrets, credentials, `.env`, private keys, payment credentials or production infrastructure settings unless explicitly requested by the project owner.

Never remove validation, authentication, authorization, logging safeguards, or tests just to make code pass.

Never deploy to production, auto-merge pull requests, delete production data, or auto-approve critical changes.

## Architecture
Keep business logic server-side when it affects security, payments, reservations, pricing, permissions or customer data.

Avoid duplicated business rules.

Respect existing folder structure, naming conventions and dependency boundaries.

## Security
Check every API and server action for:

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Input validation
- Error handling
- Sensitive data exposure
- Unsafe database writes
- Injection risks
- XSS risks
- Webhook validation where applicable

## Design System
Reuse existing components, tokens, spacing, typography, colors and layout primitives.

Do not create duplicate buttons, cards, inputs, modals or alerts when a shared component exists.

Keep UI responsive, accessible and visually consistent.

## Testing
For every risky change, add or update tests.

Prioritize tests for:

- Auth
- Permissions
- Reservation rules
- Pricing
- Checkout
- Admin actions
- API validation
- Error states

Run when validating fixes: `npm run lint`, `npm run test`, `npm run build` (and `npm run type-check` when available).

## Output
When explaining changes, always include:

- What changed
- Why it changed
- Risk
- Validation
- Next recommended step
